= Decoy effect =

== Overview ==
The '''decoy effect''' or '''attraction effect''' occurs when adding an asymmetrically dominated option changes preferences between existing alternatives.

== Context dependence ==
If option C is clearly worse than option A on relevant dimensions but not similarly dominated by B, introducing C can increase selection of A relative to B.

== Significance ==
The effect challenges simple models in which preference between A and B is independent of irrelevant alternatives. Its magnitude depends on task design and choice representation.

== Choice-set representation and model tests ==
The decoy effect is relevant to [[Related to::Decision theory|computational models of choice]] because it asks whether preferences over two existing options remain stable when the representation of the available option set changes. Let A and B be alternatives, and add C that is less attractive than A on the measured attributes but is not comparably dominated by B. The experimental question is whether selecting A becomes more likely even though A itself has not changed.

This can challenge '''regularity''', the condition that adding another possible selection cannot increase the probability assigned to a pre-existing alternative. A model or experiment should make the choice attributes, definition of dominance, comparison probabilities and sampling uncertainty explicit. The effect is context-dependent; it is not a theorem that every added dominated option shifts behavior.

In BITwiki the computational subject is the '''representation of a choice set and the behavior of a choice rule''', not a marketing slogan. The classic finding concerns human choice; an artificial agent may or may not show an analogous pattern depending on its algorithm and inputs.

